Fail a framework-backed block open cleanly when the backend is unavailable.

A framework-backed block backend (io_uring) attaches its framework inside
evpl_block_open_device, and that attach runs the framework's availability probe
lazily.  When the probe reports the backend unavailable -- io_uring_setup
returning ENOSYS under a container's default seccomp profile, a pre-5.1 kernel,
or io_uring disabled -- it leaves framework_private NULL, yet open_device still
completed as success.  The per-thread ring was then never created, so the first
read/write dispatched into the backend and dereferenced a NULL framework
context (a crash deferred from open to first I/O).

Check the probe verdict once the attach has produced it (after
protocol_private_data is read, not at the earlier unregistered-protocol branch
where framework_private is still NULL on every host): when the protocol is
framework-backed and its framework did not come up, complete the open with
ENXIO.  This is the same uniform asynchronous failure already used for an
unregistered backend, and it lets every libevpl block consumer fail the open
cleanly instead of crashing on first use.  Non-framework backends (pread) and
backends whose probe passed are unaffected.

diff --git a/src/core/block.c b/src/core/block.c
index 1b16df6..7e897b8 100644
--- a/src/core/block.c
+++ b/src/core/block.c
@@ -233,6 +233,20 @@ evpl_block_open_device(
         protocol_private_data = NULL;
     }

+    /* A framework-backed block backend (io_uring) attaches its framework just
+     * above, and that attach runs the framework's availability probe lazily:
+     * framework_private stays NULL when the backend is framework-unavailable
+     * here -- io_uring_setup returning ENOSYS under a container's seccomp
+     * profile, a pre-5.1 kernel, or io_uring disabled.  Opening anyway would
+     * succeed now and defer a NULL-context dereference to the first
+     * read/write; fail the open cleanly instead, the same uniform asynchronous
+     * failure used for an unregistered backend above. */
+    if (protocol->framework && !protocol_private_data) {
+        request->status = ENXIO;
+        evpl_defer(evpl, &request->deferral);
+        return;
+    }
+
     protocol->open_device(evpl, request->uri, protocol_private_data,
                           evpl_block_open_complete, request);
 } /* evpl_block_open_device */
